# Contrarian Brief — Quiet-by-Design Standards for MWAA Terminals

Stage 1 independent research. The Council's active thesis is that MWAA should adopt a common "quiet-by-design" standard governing acoustics, PA zoning, information systems, and operational controls at IAD and DCA. This brief argues, on the merits, that the standard as framed is the wrong instrument at the wrong time for the wrong problem.

Nothing here is a strawman. If the thesis cannot survive this brief, it does not deserve to become MWAA policy.

---

## 1. The counterargument, in eight bullets

- **Quiet is a solution masquerading as a diagnosis.** The stage-zero packet establishes no evidence that MWAA passengers, employees, or airlines have identified announcement volume or terminal acoustics as a top-quartile complaint at IAD or DCA. The thesis begins with the answer and reasons backward.
- **The peer evidence is soft.** London City (~5M pax/year, single terminal, no international transfer, high-frequency business flyers), Helsinki (subjective Finavia self-report, no published intelligibility data), and Schiphol (which never actually eliminated gate or emergency announcements) are not comparables to a 29M-passenger international hub in the Saarinen envelope or to a 25M-passenger constrained legacy terminal at DCA.
- **Digital-first design excludes the passengers who need help most.** IAD carried 10.5M international passengers in 2025; DCA absorbed five new long-haul transcontinental peaks under the 2024 FAA Reauthorization. A screen-and-app operating model penalizes non-English speakers, first-time flyers, older adults, low-vision travelers, and travelers without US data plans — exactly the passenger cohorts a public-sector authority is obligated to serve.
- **NFPA 72 and the ACAA already set the effective floor.** Any code-compliant terminal in Virginia or DC must hit STI ≥ 0.45 (avg ≥ 0.50) at 90% of measurement points in every acoustically distinguishable space, must provide redundant visual notification, and must communicate accessibly in aural and visual modes. The marginal benefit of a bespoke MWAA quiet standard, on top of compliance, is smaller than the thesis suggests.
- **A common IAD/DCA standard is a category error.** Saarinen's IAD main terminal, DCA Terminal 1 (Historic District, 1941 Bolling), César Pelli's Terminal 2, HOK's future Tier 2/3 concourses, and Turner-PGAL's Concourse E have irreconcilable geometries, ceiling heights, materials, and preservation regimes. A truly common standard forces the lowest-common-denominator or generates so many terminal-specific exceptions that the "common" element is nominal.
- **The timing claim inverts the actual cost curve.** MWAA's IAD program is entering active schematic design against a $22.5B, decade-long transformation. Concourse E opens September 2026. Introducing a new Board-adopted design standard mid-flight is when rework, procurement re-scoping, and consultant change orders are most expensive, not least.
- **The 2025 Airline Use Agreement is a live constraint.** Standards that materially affect hold-room PA, gate paging, or airline-controlled audio can trigger majority-in-interest consultation and consent under the 15-year agreement effective January 1, 2025 — bundled with the original $9B program, now overtaken by the July 29, 2026 $22.5B announcement whose airline-consent posture is not yet public.
- **The right instrument is smaller than a design standard.** An announcement-governance policy (who may page, when, in what script), enforcement of existing MWAA Design Manual and NFPA 72 provisions during commissioning, and a Concourse E pilot with measured before/after intelligibility, missed-boarding, and complaint data would produce more defensible policy at a fraction of the political and procurement cost.

