## 2. Evidence

### 2.1 The peer benchmarks the thesis relies on are thinner than they look

**London City's "silent airport" runs on a passenger profile MWAA does not have.** London City's own policy statement says "no tannoy announcements are made throughout the departure lounge" except for emergencies, and the airport explicitly credits "the increased adoption of smartphones" for making the model work [Source: https://www.londoncityairport.com/at-the-airport/need-to-know/airport-policies/silent-policy]. London City is a small, single-terminal airport dominated by business-class point-to-point flyers on short-haul European sectors — not a 29M-passenger international hub with an 8-concourse footprint and an incoming $22.5B rebuild.

**Helsinki's evidence is self-reported and subjective.** Finavia's own account of the Helsinki-Vantaa quiet-airport policy, in place since 2015, characterizes the study as showing "people found the concept more positive than neutral" and that it "lower[s] stress levels" — the language of survey mood, not measured intelligibility, missed-boarding rates, or accessibility outcomes [Source: https://www.finavia.fi/en/airports/helsinki-airport/airport/services-facilities/quiet-room]. The commonly cited industry piece on "silent" airports characterizes London City's move as "essential announcements" only, and quotes concerns that "if more people miss their flights, the policy could backfire" [Source: https://airport.nridigital.com/air_mar18/shhhh_is_the_day_of_the_airport_announcer_over].

**Schiphol never fully eliminated announcements.** The industry framing that Schiphol "ended nearly all announcements" is caveated in the same reporting: gate-area and emergency announcements continue, and the visible move to silence was principally a public-area, non-boarding paging reduction [Source: https://news.schiphol.com/silence-at-amsterdam-airport-schiphol/]. It is not a template for eliminating a paging discipline; it is a template for governing which announcements are allowed where.

### 2.2 Existing federal codes already require most of what a "quiet standard" would specify

**NFPA 72 Chapter 24 sets a hard intelligibility floor.** Emergency communication systems must achieve a Speech Transmission Index of not less than 0.45 at 90% of measurement locations in each acoustically distinguishable space, with an average STI of not less than 0.50, and any voice-emergency system that meets audibility but not intelligibility is non-compliant [Source: https://www.suppressionsystems.com/voice-intelligibility-in-mass-notification-systems/]. Every mass-notification designer must already meet these thresholds; a bespoke "quiet-by-design" ambient sound target below the NFPA floor is either redundant with the code or in tension with it.

**The Air Carrier Access Act requires accessible aural and visual redundancy.** Federal rulemaking under the ACAA obligates carriers and by extension airports to ensure "passengers with disabilities receive better access to airport facilities, including the provision of visually and tactilely accessible announcements and full and equal access to aural communications" [Source: https://www.congress.gov/bill/118th-congress/senate-bill/545/text]. A specification that reduces aural information without proven equivalence in visual, tactile, and staffed channels is a legal risk under ACAA and ADA Title II.

**TSA and DHS impose non-negotiable public-area announcements.** 49 CFR Part 1520 categorizes airport security programs as Sensitive Security Information; airport operators must display and, where directed, announce security-related information in public areas at TSA's direction [Source: https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-49/subtitle-B/chapter-XII/subchapter-C/part-1542]. A quiet standard cannot override SSI-driven paging obligations; it can only channel them.

### 2.3 The passenger profile at IAD and DCA is wrong for a screen-only model

**ACRP Research Report 177 documents that aging travelers, people with cognitive and sensory disabilities, and people with limited English fluency rely on multimodal — including auditory — wayfinding, and that reducing any one channel disproportionately harms these cohorts.** The report was developed specifically to give operators tools to audit accessibility performance and is grounded in universal design [Source: https://nap.nationalacademies.org/catalog/24930/enhancing-airport-wayfinding-for-aging-travelers-and-persons-with-disabilities].

**Advocacy and practitioner literature is explicit that audio announcements are essential for blind and low-vision travelers.** Practitioner guidance from disability-services organizations states that "providing timely access to all flight information via audio announcements is critical for passengers with vision loss" [Source: https://connect.goodmaps.com/docs/airport-accessibility/]. A design standard that measures success by reducing announcements is directly adverse to this population unless it funds compensating investment (staffed navigation, tactile signage, wearable audio channels) that the thesis does not price.

### 2.4 The cost claim about "specifying early is cheaper" is a red herring at MWAA in 2026

**Airport capital construction cost inflation is at historic highs and program schedules have compressed.** The FAA's estimated cost of eligible NPIAS projects rose 55% between the 2020 and 2024 reports, from $43.6B to $67.5B [Source: https://dwuconsulting.com/dwu-ai/airport-construction-cost-inflation]. Sector-wide, "a 38% cost escalation since 2014 means that the same scope of work completed in 2024 costs approximately $190 million more than its 2014 equivalent" for a large hub with a $500M five-year capital program [Source: https://dwuconsulting.com/dwu-ai/airport-construction-cost-inflation]. In this cost environment, introducing a new Board-adopted design standard that touches acoustics, PA infrastructure, FIDS, and tenant systems mid-program adds change-order risk to a $22.5B envelope that will already run against inflation. The thesis's cost logic ("specify early, save money") assumes a greenfield. MWAA is not a greenfield: Concourse E is in construction, Tier 2/3 are in design, DCA is a brownfield with historic-district constraints, and the IAD program is being restructured under a July 2026 announcement whose airline-consent posture is not yet public (§4–5 of the airport-context packet).

**CPE is already forecast to rise materially before the standard is even scored.** MWAA's 2025 Official Statement projected cost per enplanement rising to $20.71 by 2030 — before the $22.5B Dulles program was announced (§3 of the airport-context packet). A quiet-by-design standard is a discretionary cost added on top. It must earn its place against seat-of-the-hall economics, not against the vibe of a Helsinki press release.

### 2.5 The airline-consent risk is real

**MWAA's 15-year Airport Use Agreement, effective January 1, 2025, includes majority-in-interest provisions on premises modifications.** Any quiet-by-design specification that materially changes hold-room PA, gate-area paging authority, or airline-controlled audio triggers those consultation and consent mechanisms (§4 of the airport-context packet). United (IAD hub carrier and formal partner in the July 2026 Dulles announcement) and American (dominant slot holder at DCA) both operate hub systems where hold-room paging is used for last-call, gate change, misconnect, and IROPS recovery. Restricting that authority without airline buy-in is not a design decision — it is an amendment to the commercial relationship.

**Missed boardings are an airline cost, not an airport cost.** Schiphol's 2022 compensation program — under which the airport paid missed-flight travelers directly — is the exception, not the rule [Source: https://news.schiphol.com/schiphol-compensates-travellers-who-missed-flight-due-to-long--queue/]. In the US contractual environment, missed boardings driven by reduced paging fall on the carrier as rebooking, misconnect, and Part 259 tarmac / customer service obligations. Airlines will bill this back through use-and-lease negotiation and passenger-experience metrics. The Council should not assume airline neutrality.

