## 3. Specific scenarios where the thesis is wrong

**Scenario A — Concourse E IROPS event, December 2027.** A winter storm cascades into a 6-hour IROPS window at IAD. Under a quiet-by-design regime that suppresses hold-room and public-area paging, non-app-using international connecting passengers, elderly domestic passengers, and low-vision travelers cluster at gate desks and airline service counters. United rebooks in bulk. Missed-connection rate rises. The airline attributes the delta to MWAA's paging regime; the use-agreement dispute is teed up at the next MII meeting.

**Scenario B — DCA transcontinental peak, summer 2028.** Following the 2024 slot exemption additions (Alaska/SAN, American/SAT, Delta/SEA, Southwest/LAS, United/SFO), DCA is peaking six long-haul departures within 90 minutes on a legacy footprint. A quiet standard applied uniformly across Terminals 1 and 2 collides with Historic District constraints on Terminal 1 acoustic treatment and with Pelli Terminal 2's design intent. MWAA either issues waivers so broad the standard is nominal or forces a specification neither terminal can execute without a preservation review.

**Scenario C — ACAA plaintiff, 2029.** A blind traveler misses an IAD boarding after gate paging is suppressed by the standard. The suit alleges the design standard itself caused disparate impact under ACAA and ADA Title II. Discovery pulls the Board resolution, the design manual amendment, and the peer-airport benchmarks. Helsinki self-reports and London City comparability testimony do not read well in front of a district judge.

**Scenario D — Bond-market reception, 2030.** MWAA prices the next senior-lien issuance against a CPE that is already projected at $20.71 (§3 of the airport-context packet) and against the $22.5B Dulles program. Investors ask what discretionary program costs have been added since the 2024 baseline. "A common acoustic and passenger-communication standard" without measurable pre-standard baseline data lands as a soft answer during a hard book.

**Scenario E — The pilot never happened.** MWAA writes a Board-adopted standard before running a Concourse E instrumented pilot. Six months post-opening, the standard is either revised (embarrassing) or defended (harder to fix). ACRP 177's audit methodology is not applied. The airport cannot prove the standard is working because it never established the baseline it needed.

