## 4. Quotes and data points the strategist must address or concede

1. **Existing code, not a new MWAA standard, drives most of the specification.** NFPA 72 already requires "a minimum intelligibility rating consistent with STI of 0.45 or higher... at least 90 percent of the measurement locations within each acoustically distinguishable space (ADS) have a measured STI of not less than 0.45 (0.65 CIS) and an average STI of not less than 0.50 STI" [Source: https://www.suppressionsystems.com/voice-intelligibility-in-mass-notification-systems/]. Concede this or explain what the MWAA standard adds beyond enforcement of the code that already binds.

2. **London City is a category-different airport.** London City's policy statement: "no tannoy announcements are made throughout the departure lounge, and customers rely on information screens to obtain flight and gate announcements" and the airport credits smartphone adoption for embedding the policy [Source: https://www.londoncityairport.com/at-the-airport/need-to-know/airport-policies/silent-policy]. IAD's 10.5M international passengers in 2025 (§6 of the airport-context packet) are not London City's short-haul business-class base. Concede the analogy or price the digital-inclusion delta.

3. **Helsinki's own account is subjective.** Finavia describes the quiet-airport study conclusion as "people found the concept more positive than neutral, and... it [lowers] stress levels" [Source: https://www.finavia.fi/en/airports/helsinki-airport/airport/services-facilities/quiet-room]. Concede that this is not measured evidence of accessibility, safety, or missed-boarding outcomes at a US hub.

4. **Federal accessibility law is a live constraint.** ACAA Amendments legislation requires "the provision of visually and tactilely accessible announcements and full and equal access to aural communications" [Source: https://www.congress.gov/bill/118th-congress/senate-bill/545/text]. Concede that the standard must fund and specify compensating aural, tactile, and staffed channels, not merely reduce the aural channel.

5. **Sector cost inflation is running against the program.** "The FAA's estimated cost of eligible U.S. airport projects increased 55% between the 2020 and 2024 NPIAS reports (from $43.6 billion to $67.5 billion), driven by construction cost inflation and expanded project scope" [Source: https://dwuconsulting.com/dwu-ai/airport-construction-cost-inflation]. Against MWAA's projected $20.71 CPE by 2030 (§3 of airport-context packet) and a $22.5B IAD program, concede that the standard must document net-benefit-per-CPE-dollar, not just design intent.

---

## 5. The concession that would make the original position defensible

The original thesis becomes credible if it (a) drops the "common IAD/DCA standard" framing in favor of a two-part instrument — an authority-wide announcement-governance and commissioning policy plus terminal-specific acoustic and PA specifications — and (b) commits to an instrumented Concourse E pilot, using ACRP 177's audit methodology and NFPA 72's STI protocol, that measures baseline and post-intervention intelligibility, accessibility, missed-boarding rate, and complaint volume before a Board-adopted standard is issued. Without those two moves, the thesis is aesthetic policy dressed as engineering, and the Council should say so.

